    <DataNode elementId="CPD-547" textLabel="3-hydroxy-3-methylglutarate" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="NPOAOTPXWNWTSH-UHFFFAOYSA-L" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="310" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, hawkins">|FRAME: EC-4.1.3.4|, the enzyme that catalyzes the final step of leucine degradation, breaks down molecules of |FRAME: 3-HYDROXY-3-METHYL-GLUTARYL-COA| into |FRAME: 3-KETOBUTYRATE| and |FRAME: ACETYL-COA|. If the enzyme is defective, |FRAME: 3-HYDROXY-3-METHYL-GLUTARYL-COA| will accumulate in the mitochondria, leading to a disruption of the esterified CoA:free CoA ratio. As a result, the 3-hydroxymethylglutaryl moiety is transferred to carnitine, forming |FRAME: CPD-27164|, which is then transferred across the inner mitochondrial membrane, where |FRAME: CPD-547| is released as the free acid. Patients with a defective |FRAME: EC-4.1.3.4| suffer from 3-Hydroxy-3-methylglutaric aciduria, a rare inborn error of metabolism (OMIM: 246450), and accumulate |FRAME: CPD-547| in the urine.</Comment>
